Firstmate fork: moot-wake guard
===============================

Stops a Pi primary from being woken for a wake whose queue rows are already
gone. The cost it removes is one full model turn that runs the drain, prints
nothing, and acknowledges nothing.

It is fork-owned and edits no upstream file. Everything lives under
fork/wake-guard/. Like fork/README, this file is plain text with no extension
on purpose: upstream's documentation-audience check requires every tracked
*.md, *.mdx, *.rst, and *.txt file to be registered in an upstream-owned
inventory, and the fork does not edit that inventory.


The defect
----------

The upstream Pi watch extension (.pi/extensions/fm-primary-pi-watch.ts)
delivers an actionable close to main without reading the wake queue, and keeps
a delivered-but-unconsumed close in a pending record that rides a session
replacement (/new, /resume, /fork, reload) and is delivered again. Consumption
is matched on message text, not on a queue row. So a wake whose rows a drain
retired in the meantime reaches main again, and main's drain prints nothing.

What this guard does NOT cover: a follow-up Pi already holds in its queue.
Pi offers no way to cancel or veto a queued extension follow-up (the input
event runs when it is sent, before_agent_start cannot veto), so a row that
still existed when the follow-up was sent is outside this guard. The live
2026-11-03 repeats were of that kind: the watcher itself emitted
check: rearm-resurface three times, about 52 seconds apart, each time with a
main-eligible row queued (the HK3/HK4/HK5 guards were present and passed), main
was busy, and its first drain then retired the row and printed nothing. The
follow-up that remains is coalescing: do not send a wake whose text is
identical to one Pi accepted and has not yet consumed. It changes delivery for
present rows, so it is deliberately not part of this guard.


The seam
--------

Before a close reaches main, the upstream extension emits one offer on the
extension event bus, FM_BRANCH_DISPATCH_EVENT ("fm-branch-supervision:dispatch",
.pi/extensions/lib/fm-branch-dispatch.ts), and when a listener calls
offer.accept() it treats the close as handled by that listener: the pending
record finishes and nothing is sent. The offer is made only after the successor
watcher is restored and the --handling-delivered handshake has run, and never
when restoration failed, so continuity and every watcher-failure alarm are
untouched. fm-pi-moot-wake.ts is a second listener on that event. The
supervision branch is the first; an offer another listener already accepted is
never touched.


The rule
--------

The guard accepts an offer only when both hold:

  1. The close is a row-backed wake kind: the watcher appends a queue row
     before it prints one of check: rearm-resurface, signal:, stale:, and
     heartbeat. Wakes that carry their own durable payload without a queue row
     (check: inactive-outcome, contribution checks, process-event results) and
     every watcher-failure alarm are never settled here.
  2. state/.wake-queue exists, reads cleanly, and holds zero bytes. A missing,
     unreadable, non-empty, or blank-line queue is not proof, so the wake is
     delivered exactly as upstream would. A queue holding any row, including
     rows reserved for the supervision branch, is left to the upstream path.

Any error inside the handler is swallowed and the offer is left declined, so a
doubt delivers. Each settled wake appends one line to state/.moot-wake.log
(bounded, best-effort; a logging failure never changes the outcome).


Activation
----------

Loading is EXPLICIT, like fork/discord/fm-pi-reply-mirror.ts: the operator-owned
launcher must pass the extension on Pi launches:

  pi -e "$home/fork/wake-guard/fm-pi-moot-wake.ts"

A launcher that already passes pi -e for fm-pi-reply-mirror.ts adds this as a
second -e. Auto-discovery is not available: a new file under the
upstream-owned .pi/extensions/ is refused by fork/bin/fm-fork-hook-check
--base, so the repository change alone activates nothing. A session restarted
without the flag silently runs without the guard; state/.moot-wake.log is the
evidence that it is active.


Tests
-----

  fork/tests/fm-fork-moot-wake.test.sh       decision cases against hand-built
                                              offers: each wake kind, every
                                              doubt, a prior acceptance,
                                              malformed offers, the log bound,
                                              and the pinned event name.
  fork/tests/fm-fork-moot-wake-e2e.test.sh   the REAL upstream extension and
                                              this extension on one fake Pi
                                              event bus: a gone-row wake is
                                              settled on replay and before its
                                              first delivery with the
                                              successor and handshake intact;
                                              a present-row wake is delivered
                                              as today; rows-less wakes and
                                              restoration failures always
                                              deliver. The unguarded baseline
                                              is the pinned reproduction: if
                                              an upstream sync fixes the
                                              defect itself, it fails and
                                              this guard can be retired.
  fork/tests/moot-wake-lib.sh and moot-wake-fake-pi.mjs are their fixtures.
